{"name":"Flat cap","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Jason_at_golf.jpg","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jason_at_golf.jpg","caption":"Flat cap","source":"Wikimedia Commons via Wikipedia"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A flat cap is a rounded hat with a low crown and a small, stiff brim at the front, usually in tweed, wool or cotton. It dates to 16th-century Northern England and has been worn across Britain and Ireland ever since.","In style terms it adds a vintage, polished look and can frame the face and hide a receding or thinning hairline while worn. It has a long list of regional names, from paddy cap to scally cap."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People wear it in place of a baseball cap, matching it with jackets, knitwear or casual outfits. It is made in tweed, wool, cotton, corduroy, linen or leather and is chosen to fit the head closely."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["A dermatology study found that small-brimmed hats, including the flat cap, give negligible sun protection except at the vertex and forehead. Wide brims are needed for cheeks and nose."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Flat-cap.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Flat-cap.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Flat-cap","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","creator":"Heron","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flat-cap.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Flat_Cap_MET_29.158.486_S.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Flat_Cap_MET_29.158.486_S.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_cont