.org/wiki/Flat_cap"},"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Flat_cap","lead":["The flat cap is a rounded cap with a small, stiff brim at the front, usually in tweed, wool or cotton, though leather, linen and corduroy are also used.[1] It emerged in 16th-century Northern England, where it was usually called a bonnet, and an English law of 1571 required most non-noble men to wear woollen caps on Sundays and holidays.[1]","As a style item it gives a vintage silhouette and frames the upper face. It is known by many names, including paddy cap in Ireland, bunnet in Scotland, scally cap, cabbie cap, golf cap and ivy cap.[1][2] Notable wearers listed by Wikipedia include David Beckham, AC/DC singer Brian Johnson and the poet Pablo Neruda.[1]","A 1992 study in the British Journal of Dermatology measured how well hats protect different parts of the head. It reported that small-brimmed hats such as the flat cap protected only the vertex and forehead, while a brim wider than 7.5 cm was needed for reasonable protection of the nose and cheeks.[3]"],"infobox":[["Type","Hat"],["Origin","Northern England, 16th century"],["Materials","Tweed, wool, cotton, leather, corduroy, linen"],["Brim","Small, stiff, at front"],["Also called","Paddy cap, scally cap, cabbie cap, ivy cap"]],"sections":[{"h":"Design","paras":["The flat cap has a rounded crown that sits low on the head and a small stiff peak. The crown is built from fabric panels and typically lined inside for comfort.[1][2] It differs from the newsboy cap, which has a fuller crown made from more panels.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"History","paras":["The style dates from the 16th century and spread across Britain and Ireland in the 19th and early 20th centuries. It also reached southern Italy, Turkey, where it replaced the fez, the Balkans and Madrid.[1] Grokipedia adds that it became associated with working-class dress and later with golf and country pursuits.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found on its appearance effects. A 1992 measurement study of sun protection found small-brimmed hats such as the flat cap gave negligible protection across most of the face.[3]"],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"16th century","event":"The style appears in Northern England as a bonnet.[1]"},{"year":"1571","event":"English law requires most non-noble men to wear woollen caps on Sundays and holidays.[1]"}],"refs":[{"title":"Flat cap","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flat_cap","source":"Wikipedia","year":null,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Flat cap","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Flat_cap","source":"Grokipedia","year":null,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Sun protection with hats","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1637687/","source":"British Journal of Dermatology","year":1992,"type":"study"}],"studies":[{"title":"Sun protection with hats","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1637687/","source":"British Journal of Dermatology","year":1992,"kind":null}],"reddit":[],"forums":[{"title":"Flame Free Confession III: Even More Flame Free (Part 1)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/flame-free-confession-iii-even-more-flame-free-part-1/264067","site":"T Nation · 10000 posts · 2020"},{"title":"The Stupid Thread 2 (Part 1)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/the-stupid-thread-2-part-1/219703","site":"T Nation · 9999 posts · 2016"},{"title":"More Trouble Than I Am Worth: Chaos Is The Plan (T3hPwnisher Log)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/more-trouble-than-i-am-worth-chaos-is-the-plan-t3hpwnisher-log/276232","site":"T Nation · 8675 posts · 2021"},{"title":"Flame Free Confession III: Even More Flame Free (Part 2)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/flame-free-confession-iii-even-more-flame-free-part-2/273731","site":"T Nation · 8490 posts · 2021"},{"title":"Per aspera ad astra (strongman Koestrizer)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/per-aspera-ad-astra-strongman-koestrizer/213205","site":"T Nation · 8404 posts · 2015"},{"title":"Coronavirus - What Happened?","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/coronavirus-what-happened/264503","site":"T Nation · 7175 posts · 2020"}]}